--TEST--
Every `match` arm is compared with its subject by `==`, so each of the seven disjoint pairings is refused at the condition that wrote it — one error per condition, never at the subject and never at the working arm beside it, `rule:expressions/disjoint-comparison-refused`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// covers: lang:expressions/match
// A `match` compares every condition with its subject by `==`, so a
// condition whose type shares no value with the subject is refused where it
// is written. Seven disjoint pairings sit here, each beside a condition that
// is fine, and each is reported once at its own condition — never at the
// subject, and never at the arms around it.
enum Rank { Bronze, Silver }

class Meter { public function constructor(public int $reading) {} }
class Gauge { public function constructor(public int $reading) {} }

int $n = 1;
string $s = "1";
bytes $b = "1" as bytes;
Rank $rank = Rank::Bronze;
Meter $meter = new Meter(1);

var $numberAgainstText = match ($n) { 1 => "a", "one" => "b", default => "c" };
var $textAgainstNumber = match ($s) { "1" => 1, 1 => 2, default => 3 };
var $textAgainstBytes = match ($s) { $b => 1, default => 0 };
var $enumAgainstInt = match ($rank) { Rank::Bronze => 1, 0 => 2, default => 3 };
var $twoClasses = match ($meter) { new Gauge(1) => 1, default => 0 };
var $presentAgainstNull = match ($n) { null => 1, default => 0 };
var $conditionIsNotBool = match (true) { $n > 0 => 1, 5 => 2, default => 3 };
echo "unreached\n";
--EXPECTF-ERROR--
error[E0466]: `int` and `string` are disjoint; no value is both, so this comparison is always false
   --> %s:19:49
   |
19 | var $numberAgainstText = match ($n) { 1 => "a", "one" => "b", default => "c" };
   |                                                 ^^^^^ compared here
   = help: convert one side deliberately and then compare — `$s == ($n as string)`

error[E0466]: `string` and `int` are disjoint; no value is both, so this comparison is always false
   --> %s:20:49
   |
20 | var $textAgainstNumber = match ($s) { "1" => 1, 1 => 2, default => 3 };
   |                                                 ^ compared here
   = help: convert one side deliberately and then compare — `$s == ($n as string)`

error[E0466]: `string` and `bytes` are disjoint; no value is both, so this comparison is always false
   --> %s:21:38
   |
21 | var $textAgainstBytes = match ($s) { $b => 1, default => 0 };
   |                                      ^^ compared here
   = help: convert one side deliberately and then compare — `$s == ($n as string)`

error[E0466]: `Rank` and `int` are disjoint; no value is both, so this comparison is always false
   --> %s:22:58
   |
22 | var $enumAgainstInt = match ($rank) { Rank::Bronze => 1, 0 => 2, default => 3 };
   |                                                          ^ compared here
   = help: an enum case carries an integer — compare `$e as int` against the number

error[E0466]: `Meter` and `Gauge` are disjoint; no value is both, so this comparison is always false
   --> %s:23:36
   |
23 | var $twoClasses = match ($meter) { new Gauge(1) => 1, default => 0 };
   |                                    ^^^^^^^^^^^^ compared here
   = help: two unrelated classes have no value in common — compare something both of them carry, such as an id property

error[E0466]: `int` cannot hold `null`, so this test is always false
   --> %s:24:40
   |
24 | var $presentAgainstNull = match ($n) { null => 1, default => 0 };
   |                                        ^^^^ compared here
   = help: declare the binding nullable — `?T` — if it is meant to be optional, or drop the test

error[E0466]: `bool` and `int` are disjoint; no value is both, so this comparison is always false
   --> %s:25:55
   |
25 | var $conditionIsNotBool = match (true) { $n > 0 => 1, 5 => 2, default => 3 };
   |                                                       ^ compared here
   = help: convert one side deliberately and then compare — `$s == ($n as string)`

error: aborting due to 7 errors
